<InsightArticle title="One blended AI price can put your shortlist in the wrong order" description="Frozen AIRH prices reveal a ranking reversal when the balance between reading and writing changes. A worked guide to comparing the bill for your own workload." date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="9 min" snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="At three output tokens per input token, two frozen AIRH records give blended prices of $1.75 and $1.95 per million tokens. At one output token per ten input tokens, their order reverses: about $0.83 and $0.50.">
<p>The first pair makes Mistral Large 4 look cheaper than Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite. The second makes Flash-Lite look cheaper. Nothing about either recorded price changed between those calculations. We changed the work being priced, from a job that writes much more than it reads to one that reads much more than it writes.</p>
<p>These are calculations from AIRH's frozen catalogue on 14 November 2026, inspected for this guide on that date. They are not fresh quotes from either vendor, a recommendation to buy either model, or evidence about answer quality. The source records label the prices as official, but a label and an import timestamp do not establish that an offer is still available. The <a href="/transparency/#workload-price-guides">full source receipt and formulas</a> explain those limits.</p>
<h2>The same two records support opposite rankings</h2>
<p>A token is a unit in the text processed by a model. In this calculation, input means tokens sent to it and output means tokens charged for its response. We do not convert words into tokens or assume that two models split a document identically. The worked numbers deliberately hold the token counts equal so that the effect of the price mix can be seen on its own.</p>
<p>The frozen Mistral row records $0.70 for a million input tokens and $2.10 for a million output tokens. The Gemini row records $0.30 and $2.50. One has the higher reading price; the other has the higher writing price. That crossing is the reason a single price can change their order. If one row had lower rates on both sides, changing the mix alone could not produce this particular reversal.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={['Output tokens per input token','Large: dollars per million combined tokens','Flash-Lite: dollars per million combined tokens']} rows={d.mix.map(r=>[r.outputPerInput,r.large,r.flashLite])} caption="Frozen catalogue rates, hypothetical equal token counts. Each row divides the bill by total input plus output tokens. Values rounded to six decimal places; these are neither live offers nor quality comparisons." />
<p>The denominator here is the combined number of tokens read and written. A denominator is simply the quantity a rate is divided by. Without that definition, a price per million tokens can look comparable while describing a different mixture. The table's first row has ten input tokens for each output token; its last row has three output tokens for each input token.</p>
<p>AIRH's existing blended-price convention uses the last of those mixtures. Its formula is input price plus three times output price, divided by four. That is a clear convention for making a compact column. It is not a measurement of your application, and the word blended does not remove the assumption.</p>
<h2>Equal reading and writing is the crossing point</h2>
<p>Let r mean output tokens divided by input tokens. The two weighted rates are (0.70 + 2.10r) divided by (1 + r), and (0.30 + 2.50r) divided by (1 + r). Setting them equal gives 0.40 = 0.40r, so r equals one. Both rates are then $1.40 per million combined tokens. This is an exact result under the recorded prices, before rounding.</p>
<p>Below that ratio, the cheaper input rate wins this narrow price comparison. Above it, the cheaper output rate wins. A team whose tasks sit near the crossing point should be especially wary of declaring a lasting winner from one average. A modest change in the balance of reading and writing could move the estimate to the other side without any vendor changing a rate.</p>
<p>The crossing says nothing about which model completes a task with fewer tokens. It also says nothing about whether either answer passes review. Those need observations from the intended application. Equal counts are useful for explaining the arithmetic; they are a poor substitute for measuring how the actual candidates behave.</p>
<h2>A mixed queue needs totals before averages</h2>
<p>Consider an application with both long-document questions and short prompts that ask for extended writing. Its overall ratio belongs to the whole queue: total charged output divided by total charged input. Averaging each request's ratio gives equal weight to small and large requests, which answers a different question. A tiny request with almost no input can have an extreme ratio without contributing much to the invoice.</p>
<p>For an estimate based on a sample, each request can carry its measured input count, measured output count and task category. Multiplying those counts by the relevant rates gives a per-request amount. The sum gives the sample's model-token bill. This method retains the units throughout and allows someone else to reproduce the calculation without trusting a headline average.</p>
<p>Where the future queue differs from the sample, category weights become explicit assumptions. A plan for mostly document questions should not quietly inherit a sample dominated by drafting. Weighting means assigning each category the share expected in the future queue. The shares need an owner and a reason, particularly when they change the apparent winner.</p>
<p>An estimate can show both the observed mixture and a second plausible mixture. That makes the consequence of a changing workload visible. Neither mixture needs to be described as typical of AI use generally. The useful question is narrower: which mix describes the work this particular application is intended to handle?</p>
<h2>The invoice still has conditions this table omits</h2>
<p>The calculation uses only the two token rates saved in the catalogue. It excludes caching, batch rates, tools, taxes and any separately charged processing. It excludes reasoning tokens as an additional category rather than guessing how a service reports them. A real invoice comparison needs the actual billing categories from the service being considered and the terms that apply to that account.</p>
<p>Likewise, an import's time is not the date a person verified an offer. Our Mistral record contains a source label saying checked on 13 November; both rows have the following day's import time. The guide preserves those strings in its frozen receipt instead of promoting them into a new verification claim. A purchasing decision needs a fresh check of the exact seller, model and service conditions.</p>
<p>The narrow comparison can still be useful before that check. It reveals that the ordering in a compact price column is conditional. A team can use the column to find candidates, then replace the convention with its own measured input and output totals. The original column need not be wrong for the team's estimate to come out differently.</p>
<p>For readers keeping a results log, meaning a written record of the trial and its outcome, the essential price evidence is the pair of rates, their source, the counts and the formula. The rank is a result of those choices. Preserving them makes a later reversal explainable instead of making it look like a mysterious change in model value.</p>
<p>The two rows in this guide are selected to demonstrate crossing prices. They are not a survey of how often rankings reverse across the catalogue. The lesson supported by the frozen example is specific: when one candidate reads more cheaply and the other writes more cheaply, the workload's balance belongs beside the ranking.</p>
<p>A useful way to challenge the ranking is to ask what quantity stays fixed. This table holds a million combined tokens fixed while the mixture changes. A monthly budget instead fixes a planned set of jobs. Both calculations can be correct, yet the amounts have different meanings. A dollar figure per million combined tokens should not be copied into a monthly forecast without multiplying it by the corresponding total usage.</p>
<p>That distinction also helps when someone presents a percentage saving. The baseline must be the same workload priced through the alternative, with the same included charges. Comparing one model's input-heavy trial against another model's writing-heavy trial would mix a workload difference with a rate difference. The frozen example keeps those apart by giving both candidates the same counts in each row and changing the mixture only between rows.</p>
</InsightArticle>
